  "levels": {
    "1": {
      "title": "1Password has a security problem",
      "text": "1Password is a tool that keeps passwords safe. On Wednesday, the company said it had a security problem. A hacker got into the GitHub account of one worker. The hacker copied source code. The hacker also saw names and email addresses of some customers. The company says the password vaults are safe. Customers do not need to do anything. But they should watch for strange emails."
    },
    "2": {
      "title": "1Password says hackers took some customer data",
      "text": "1Password, a company that makes password software, said on Wednesday that it had a security incident. An unknown attacker got into the GitHub account of an employee. The company began to investigate suspicious activity on November 19, 2026.\n\nThe attacker copied source code and saw a limited amount of customer information from some customers, such as names and email addresses. The company says that no vault content, passwords or recovery keys were reached.\n\n1Password closed the account, replaced the stolen token and changed internal passwords. It also made the codes in some emails longer, from six digits to ten characters. Customers do not need to act, but they should be careful with unexpected phishing emails."
    },
    "3": {
      "title": "1Password discloses breach of backend systems through a GitHub account",
      "text": "1Password, the Toronto-based password manager, disclosed on Wednesday that an unknown attacker had gained access to an employee's corporate GitHub account and used it to reach some of the company's backend systems. The company began investigating suspicious activity on November 19, 2026.\n\nAccording to 1Password, the attacker copied source code from private repositories, entered its CI/CD system and downloaded a limited amount of customer account information, including names and email addresses, from a subset of customers. A small number of users were affected and were notified individually.\n\nThe company stressed that no vault content, secrets, recovery keys or the keys that protect them were accessed. It said vault secrets are encrypted with keys that only the user holds. Source code, it added, contains no user secrets.\n\nAs a response, 1Password disabled the affected GitHub account, revoked the stolen token, hardened its controls and rotated internal credentials. It also lengthened the codes in invitation and recovery emails from six digits to ten characters. Customers are not required to act but should stay alert to phishing."
    }
  },
